<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd al-Haqq II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1419">1419</a>–<a href="/years/1465">1465</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd al-Haqq II was the last Marinid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, proclaimed as a baby in 1420 and kept under the control of Wattasid regents for most of his reign. In 1459 he tried to free himself by massacring the Wattasids, but his government grew unpopular, and he was killed in a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1465. The surviving Wattasids later founded their own sultanate.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abd al-Haqq was the son of Sultan Abu Said Uthman III, whose failed attempt in 1419 to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> from the Portuguese unsettled the Marinid state. The instability ended in a coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1420 in which Abu Said was assassinated, leaving his one-year-old son as heir while other pretenders came forward. Abu Zakariya Yahya al-Wattasi, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a>, hurried to seize the royal palace of Fez, proclaimed the orphaned child sultan and made himself regent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> quickly fell into disorder, and by 1423 Abu Zakariya had become the effective ruler. When Abd al-Haqq came of age in 1437, the regent refused to give up the regency. That year a Portuguese attempt to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a> by siege failed, raising Moroccan morale and the prestige of the sharifs who had led the defence, and Abu Zakariya used the victory to strengthen his own hold. In January 1438, under his administration, the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-ii">Idris II</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>, was rediscovered and became an important place of pilgrimage.</p><p>The regency stayed in the Wattasid family. Abu Zakariya was succeeded in 1448 by his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf">Ali ibn Yusuf</a>, who was followed in 1458 by Abu Zakariya's son, Yahya ibn Abi Zakariya. Through all these years Abd al-Haqq was sultan in name and held no power. He was murdered on 14 August 1465 during the Moroccan revolt of that year, and Marinid rule ended with him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd al-Haqq II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Abd al-Haqq II</b> became sultan in 1420 at the age of one, and the Wattasid viziers ruled as regents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1448" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abd al-Haqq II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4646 4647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4646 4647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1448">What was here in 1448? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
